  "mic": "XDUB",
  "name": "Euronext Dublin",
  "note": "2010-2024 sourced from exchange_calendars only (backfill; not independently verified). 2025-2027: exchange_calendars + exchange_publications (the exchange's calendar, published to 2027). 2028-2029: predicted from exchange_calendars and the exchange's unchanged pattern; not yet published, although most entries cite the calendar page. Close-time convention: the registry uses 16:30 (full days) and 12:30 (half days) for 2025-2029, while exchange_calendars uses 16:28 and 12:28 and so do the backfilled 2010-2024 half days (12:28). This is not rounding in the library: Euronext's half-day notice gives Dublin equities a close between 13:28 and 13:30 CET (12:28-12:30 Dublin time), a two-minute window, so the registry holds the end of the window and the library its start. Rechecked 2026-11-19 (4c-R3) against Euronext's trading-hours-and-holidays page and its 2027 holiday calendar. Fixed: (1) 2027-12-28 (Tue) is closed but unconfirmed (maintainer decision, 4c-R3.5, reversing 4c-R3): Irish law moves St Stephen's Day (Sun 26 Dec 2027) to Tue 28 Dec; in 2021, the same weekday layout, Euronext closed Dublin on both 27 and 28 December (Euronext's 2021 holiday-calendar release, which this entry cites); and both exchange_calendars and pandas_market_calendars close it. Euronext's 2027 holiday calendar lists only Mon 27 December, which is the reason for doubt. The 2025-2027 coverage span marks it unconfirmed with recheck_date 2027-11-30: re-check against Euronext Dublin's end-of-2027 notice and open the day only if Euronext says explicitly that it trades. (2) When Dec 24 or Dec 31 falls on a weekend, Euronext Dublin holds the half day on the preceding Friday (2022-12-23, 2022-12-30, 2023-12-22, 2023-12-29 in Euronext's tables); 2028-12-22 and 2028-12-29 added as predicted half days, as exchange_calendars has them. (3) Half-day time convention (4c-R3 decision: close = end of the closing auction): Euronext states Dublin equities close between 12:28 and 12:30 Dublin time on half days (random end), so the close is 12:30, as the 2025+ entries already had; the 2022-2024 half days (12:28 from exchange_calendars) are now 12:30, citing Euronext, whose statement covers those years. Earlier half days: see the 4c-R3.5 close-convention sentence below (2019-2021 12:30, 2010-2018 12:28). The full-day 16:30 is likewise the end of the 16:28-16:30 closing window. Checked, no change: Irish May Bank Holiday closures (2025-05-05, 2026-05-04, 2027-05-03), 2026-12-28 and 2027-12-27 match Euronext; no COVID-19 closure; hours 08:00-16:30 Dublin throughout. Close convention (ADR 0013, 4c-R3.5): half-day close = end of the closing auction. Euronext Dublin moved from the ISE's Xetra (Deutsche Börse) platform to Euronext's Optiq on 2019-02-04 (Euronext press release of 4 February 2019, https://www.euronext.com/en/about/media/euronext-press-releases/euronext-dublin-transitions-irish-listed-companies-optiq), not in 2021 as the 4c-R3 note said. Half days since then: 2019 (24 and 31 Dec): Euronext's trading hours & holidays page, 'Calendar of business days 2019' (https://live.euronext.com/en/resources/trading-hours-holidays), gives Dublin equities closing between 13:28 and 13:30 CET (12:28-12:30 Dublin time) and ETFs at 13:00 Dublin, so 12:30 (4c-R4; 4c-R3.5 had 13:05 from the October 2018 release's 'all instruments closing by 14:05 CET', which is an upper bound, not an auction end); 2020 and 2021: Euronext's holiday-calendar releases give the same 12:28-12:30 Dublin window, so 12:30, as for 2022+. Close (4c8, resolved): Euronext's Appendix to Instructions 4-01/4-03, sheet 'Appendix to 4-01 Manual OPTQ', effective 3 August 2026 (https://www.euronext.com/sites/default/files/2026-07/appendix%20to%20Euronext%20Instructions%204-01%204-03%20Trading%20Manuals_0.xlsx; the version effective 23 November 2026 has the same Dublin rows), gives every Dublin equity trading group in continuous mode (9A, 9B, 9C, 9D, 9F, 9G, 9J, 9O, 9P, and JQ in the November version), in CET: continuous trading '09:00 Random - 17:28', closing uncrossing '17:30 Random' and Trading at Last '17:30 - 17:40'. Its notes define '17:30 Random' as a closing uncrossing that randomly occurs between 17:30:00 and 17:30:30 CET. So 16:28-16:30 Dublin time is the closing call and the scheduled uncross is 16:30 Dublin; regular_hours.close 16:30 is the end of the closing auction under ADR 0013 (the 30-second random end is ignored), and the 4c-R5 question whether the uncross was scheduled at 16:28 is answered no. Half days: the Appendix has no half-day rows (those are in Euronext's end-of-year appendix, not read); Euronext's statement that Dublin equities 'close between 12:28 and 12:30' has the same two-minute closing-call shape, so 12:30 is kept as the half-day uncross. The 2019-02-04 unconfirmed marker is removed. 2010-2018 half days keep exchange_calendars' 12:28: the ISE Xetra era, for which no half-day schedule was found.",
  "regular_hours": {
    "close": "16:30",
    "open": "08:00"
  },
  "sessions": [
